  translation: "1. The blessed Lord said: I taught this imperishable yoga to Vivasvat. Vivasvat told it to Manu, and Manu told it to Ikshvaku.\n2. The royal sages knew this yoga, received in this way through the succession. In this world that yoga has been lost through the long lapse of time, O scorcher of the foe.\n3. This same ancient yoga I have now taught to you. You are devoted to me, and you are my friend; for this is the highest secret.\n4. Arjuna said: Your birth was later, and the birth of Vivasvat was earlier. How am I to understand that you taught this in the beginning?\n5. The blessed Lord said: Many of my births have passed, Arjuna, and many of yours. I know them all. You do not know them, O scorcher of the foe.\n6. Though I am unborn and my nature does not waste away, and though I am the lord of beings, I take charge of my own nature and come to be by my own maya.\n7. Whenever dharma declines, O Bharata, and adharma rises, then I bring myself forth.\n8. For the protection of the good, for the destruction of those who do evil, and to set dharma firm, I come to be in age after age.\n9. He who knows my birth and my action as divine, and knows them as they truly are, does not go on to another birth when he leaves the body. He comes to me, Arjuna.\n10. Many, from whom passion, fear, and anger have gone, who are absorbed in me and have taken refuge in me, have been made pure by the austerity of knowledge and have reached my state.\n11. In whatever way people resort to me, in that same way I favor them. Men follow my path in every way, O son of Pritha.\n12. Those who want the success of their actions sacrifice here to the gods. For in the human world, success born of action comes quickly.\n13. The four orders were brought forth by me, according to the division of the qualities and of actions. Though I am the maker of that, know me as one who does not act, and as unchanging.\n14. Actions do not stain me, and I have no craving for the fruit of action. He who knows me in this way is not bound by actions.\n15. Knowing this, those who sought release in the past also did action. Therefore do action yourself. It was done by the earlier ones long before.\n16. What is action, and what is non-action? Even the wise are confused about this. I shall declare that action to you. Knowing it, you will be freed from what is unholy.\n17. Even of action there is something to be known, and of mis-action, and of non-action. The course of action is deep.\n18. He who sees non-action in action, and action in non-action, is wise among men. He is a yogin, one who has done all that is to be done.\n19. He whose every undertaking is free of desire and of the resolve that gives rise to it, and whose actions have been burned by the fire of knowledge: him those who know call wise.\n20. Having given up the claim of being the doer in action, and attachment to its fruit, always content and with nothing he leans on, he does nothing at all, even when he is fully engaged in action.\n21. One from whom wishes have gone, whose mind and body are held in check, and by whom every acquisition has been given up, does not incur a stain when he does only the action that keeps the body alive.\n22. Content with a gain that comes of itself, unshaken by the pairs and free of envy, he is the same when that gain comes and when it does not. Even while doing the act, he is not bound.\n23. For the man whose attachment has withdrawn, who is free, whose mind rests in knowledge, and who acts for the sake of sacrifice, action dissolves together with its fruit.\n24. The offering is Brahman, the oblation is Brahman, and Brahman offers it into the fire that is Brahman. Brahman itself is what he reaches whose meditation is that the action is Brahman.\n25. Some ritualists attend only to sacrifice offered to the gods. Others offer the sacrifice into the fire of Brahman by the sacrifice itself.\n26. Some offer hearing and the other senses into the fires of restraint. Another offers sound and the other objects into the fires that are the senses.\n27. Others cast all the actions of the senses, and the actions of the vital breaths, into the fire of the yoga of self-restraint, kindled by knowledge.\n28. There are men whose sacrifice is a gift of wealth, men whose sacrifice is austerity, and men whose sacrifice is yoga; and others, men of effort whose sacrifice is recitation and knowledge, their vows made keen.\n29. Others, given over to restraint of the breath, offer the outgoing breath into the downward breath and the downward breath into the outgoing breath, and stop those two paths.\n30. Others, keeping their food limited, offer the breaths into the breath. All of these know sacrifice, and the sacrifices have destroyed their stain.\n31. Those who eat what remains of the sacrifice go to the eternal Brahman. Even this world is not for the man who offers none of these, O best of the Kurus. How could another, higher, end be his?\n32. Many kinds of sacrifice are spread thus at the door of the Veda. Know them all as born of action. Knowing this, you will be released.\n33. The sacrifice of knowledge is better than a sacrifice made of material things, O scorcher of the foe. All action, the whole of it, comes to rest in knowledge, O son of Pritha.\n34. Learn that by a full prostration, by questioning, and by service. Those who know, and who see the truth, will teach you that knowledge.\n35. Knowing that, you will not fall into confusion again as you have now, O son of Pandu. By it you will see every being in the self, and in Me.\n36. Even if you are the one who has done the most wrong of all who do wrong, you will cross the whole of that wrong by the raft of knowledge alone.\n37. As a well-kindled fire makes fuel into ash, O Arjuna, so the fire of knowledge makes all actions into ash.\n38. Nothing here purifies as knowledge does. The man himself, made fit by yoga, finds that knowledge in himself in time.\n39. The man of faith, intent on the means and with his senses drawn in, obtains knowledge. Having gained it, he reaches the highest peace before long.\n40. The ignorant man, the man without faith, and the man whose mind is doubt all perish. For the doubter there is neither this world, nor the next, nor happiness.\n41. Actions do not bind the attentive man who has laid action down by yoga and whose doubt knowledge has cut, O Dhananjaya.\n42. Therefore cut this doubt, born of ignorance and seated in the heart, with the sword of your own knowledge. Stand in yoga, and rise, O Bharata.",
